<ClinicNote slug="shoulder-pain-stiff-or-unstable">
  <h2>A mobility problem: "It won't go there"</h2>
  <p>The shoulder is stiff, and it stops early whether you lift it yourself or someone else lifts it for you. Turning the arm outwards is usually the first thing lost. The classic example is frozen shoulder, most common between 40 and 60 and more likely with diabetes or thyroid conditions. It often passes through painful, stiff and recovery phases, and without treatment it can take one to three years [6].</p>
  <h2>A stability problem: "It goes there, but doesn't stay put"</h2>
  <p>The range is there, but the ball doesn't stay centred in the socket. You might feel it slip, clunk or "go dead", especially overhead. This can follow a dislocation, or come on without injury in people with naturally flexible joints. After a first dislocation, recurrence is common, especially in younger people:</p>
  <blockquote><p>"Recurrent instability following a first-time traumatic anterior shoulder dislocation was 39%."</p><cite>Olds et al., Br J Sports Med, 2015 [7]</cite></blockquote>
  <h2>The most common one: overloaded</h2>
  <p>Rotator cuff related shoulder pain is a load problem more than either of the above: the shoulder moves and stays put, but the tendons can't currently handle what you're asking of them. Scans often show cuff changes, but those are common in shoulders that don't hurt: in one population study, tears without symptoms were twice as common as tears with symptoms [4]. Exercise is the first-line treatment [3], and placebo-controlled surgery trials have found shaving bone off the shoulder offers no extra benefit:</p>
  <blockquote><p>"Surgical decompression appeared to offer no extra benefit over arthroscopy only."</p><cite>Beard et al. (CSAW trial), The Lancet, 2018 [1]</cite></blockquote>
  <h2>How I can help</h2>
  <p>The assessment works out which pattern you have (often it's a mix). Stiff shoulders get hands-on mobilisation and range work, plus a referral for an injection when it's warranted. In a large UK trial, physio plus an injection held up well against surgery [5]. Unstable shoulders get progressive strength and control training, which has strong results for non-injury instability [8]. Overloaded shoulders get load management and a progressive strength plan. In every case, you'll know what we're doing and why.</p>

